        "action": "stop",
        "modifications": {},
        "rationale": "[recovery-agent diagnosis via claude-opus-5[1m]] ROOT CAUSE: The TG_REVIEW label says 'breakpoint_ambiguity', but the grid is not too coarse to place the breakpoint. thermal.json shows breakpoint_spread_K=0.0 over 1 split, so there are no competing splits. extract_thermal.py:1119 sets the value as max(spread, 2*sigma), and the 180.9 K comes entirely from 2 x tg_uncertainty_K=90.5 K. The bilinear branches are nearly parallel (a_glassy=-2.87e-4, a_rubbery=-3.89e-4, ratio 1.35), so their crossing point is loose. The rubbery bins (600-710 K) are noisy: many plateau bins have n_points 3-15, relax_warning appears throughout 600-710 K, and density does not fall steadily with T (e.g. 585 K 1.1033 vs 590 K 1.0889; 690 K 1.0287 vs 695 K 1.0566). The primary hyperbola fit was degenerate (Tg=525 K) and was swapped for bilinear (401.1 K, R2=0.9596). That value sits well below both experiment (PSU 463 K) and PSU_1's validated Tg. The glassy branch (250-400 K, n=200/bin) is clean.\n\nWHY THE RIGHT LEVER IS NOT CLEAR: (1) tg_breakpoint (halve tg_t_step_K) is the wrong lever: spread is already 0, and 20/(100 K/ns x 1 fs) = 200000 steps/T is exactly PSFO's tg_min_steps_per_T floor, so the remedy declines by construction. remedy_history is empty, which is consistent with that. (2) This is not a protocol failure. PSU_1 ran the same class protocol (PCFF, 100 K/ns, dt 1 fs, PSFO sizing) and validated Tg=481.3 K with a GOOD fit (R2=0.9849) (system_characterization_cache.json). The same protocol gave a clean fit on another trajectory, so this looks like trajectory/seed noise on a DP=12 rigid aromatic cell. It matches the seed-noise pattern already recorded for PKTN/PSTR. (3) Lowering tg_rate_K_per_ns (e.g. 100 -> 50) carries EXTRA_INVALIDATION to cooling. That re-runs cooling (~2.7 h wall this run) plus a ~10 h sweep, roughly 13 h. At best it cuts per-point noise by about sqrt(2), taking 2*sigma to ~128 K, against a gate of 20 K, a 9x shortfall. I could not get a verdict from remedy_economics.py: both invocations were blocked by the permission layer. Supplied args: --gate-class C --lever cooling_rate_K_per_ns lower --history 100:180.9 --next-lever 50 --target-floor 20 --physical-target 463 --sem 90.5 --last-rung-hours 5.08 --cost-exponent -1. With one history point it could only return 'spend_limit: one rung', and the sqrt(2) arithmetic points toward STOP_ANNOTATE. (4) Changing velocity_seed maps to 'equilibration' (workflow_engine.py:121). That re-runs equilibration (~6.3 h), cooling and thermal, which amounts to re-rolling the gate on a different trajectory rather than fixing a cause. (5) retry: nothing transient happened. The attempt finished normally, and every per-T point was PASS/stable.\n\nDECISION NEEDED FROM A HUMAN: either (a) accept PSU_2's Tg as not reportable, with an annotation (sigma=90.5 K, near-parallel branches, noisy melt branch, and RIGID_BACKBONE_CHAIN_LENGTH_BIAS at DP=12, which the plan already flagged), and take Tg from PSU_1's validated 481.3 K while continuing to the mechanical stage; or (b) knowingly spend about 13 GPU-h on tg_rate_K_per_ns=50, which is unlikely to bring 2*sigma under 20 K. The planner's own note (critique finding #5) also left the rate untouched on purpose, to test the retired slowest_rate fallback, so any rate change is a policy choice."
      },
